import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var a={projectile:{theory:{concept:"[[Projectile motion is the motion of a body thrown at an angle to the horizontal that then moves under gravity alone (air resistance neglected); its path is a parabola.]] The motion splits into two independent parts. Horizontally no force acts, so the body moves uniformly at the constant speed v\u2080 \xB7 cos \u03B8. Vertically gravity gives it a constant downward acceleration g, so the vertical speed changes just as in free fall. Air resistance shortens and bends the path; without it, a body launched from the ground flies furthest at 45\xB0."},section:{independent:{title:"Two motions in one",text:"[[The horizontal and the vertical motion do not disturb each other.]] Horizontally nothing pushes or pulls the ball, so it keeps a constant speed v\u2080\xB7cos \u03B8. Vertically gravity slows it on the way up and speeds it on the way down, exactly as if it had been thrown straight up with v\u2080\xB7sin \u03B8. A bullet fired horizontally and a bullet dropped from the same height hit the ground at the same moment."},angle:{title:"Which angle goes furthest?",text:"On flat ground the range is R = v\u2080\xB2 \xB7 sin(2\u03B8) / g. It is largest at 45\xB0, and two angles that add up to 90\xB0 \u2014 like 30\xB0 and 60\xB0 \u2014 land at the same place: the steep one flies higher and longer, the flat one faster and lower. Doubling the launch speed makes the range four times longer."},heightTime:{title:"How high and how long",text:"[[The ball rises while its vertical speed is upwards and reaches the top when it is zero, after t = v\u2080\xB7sin \u03B8 / g, at the height H = (v\u2080\xB7sin \u03B8)\xB2 / (2g).]] On flat ground the way down takes as long as the way up. At the top the ball is still moving \u2014 horizontally \u2014 and its acceleration is still g, straight down."},air:{title:"Air changes everything",text:"In real air the ball is slowed down, mostly while it is fast. The path is no longer a symmetric parabola: it comes down more steeply than it went up, the range is shorter, and the best angle is below 45\xB0. A football or a golf ball can fly only half as far as in a vacuum. Spin adds more effects, such as a curving free kick."},galileo:{title:"Galileo and the parabola",text:"For centuries people thought a cannonball flies straight, then suddenly falls. In his book Two New Sciences (1638) Galileo Galilei showed that the path is a parabola, because the even horizontal motion and the accelerated vertical fall simply add up. It was one of the first times mathematics described a motion exactly."},everyday:{title:"Projectiles everywhere",text:"A basketball shot, a long jumper, the water of a fountain and a stone skipped across a pond all follow the same rules. Water from a garden hose shows the whole parabola at once. Athletes in the long jump take off at about 20\xB0, not 45\xB0, because they cannot run fast and jump steeply at the same time."},check:{title:"Check your understanding",text:"At which angle does a ball go furthest on flat ground? (45\xB0, without air.) Which angle lands at the same place as 30\xB0? (60\xB0.) The launch speed doubles. What happens to the range? (It becomes four times longer.) At the top of the path, is the speed zero? (No \u2014 only the vertical part is.) Test each answer in the simulation."}},formula:{range:"Range on flat ground",x:"Horizontal position",y:"Vertical position",maxHeight:"Maximum height",drag:"Air drag force"},assumption:{noAir:"Air resistance is ignored unless you switch it on (Advanced level).",flatGround:"The ground is flat and the ball stops when it lands.",pointMass:"The ball is treated as a point; its spin is ignored. It is drawn larger than real size so you can see it.",uniformGravity:"Gravity is the same everywhere in the experiment.",cannon:"The cannon is not to scale: it keeps the same size on screen at any zoom. The path starts at the barrel's pivot, at the height h\u2080; when h\u2080 is small, the cannon stands in a pit so that the pivot is exactly at that height."},param:{speed:"Launch speed",angle:"Launch angle",gravity:"Gravity",mass:"Ball mass",launchHeight:"Launch height",airResistance:"Air resistance",dragCoefficient:"Drag coefficient",timeStep:"Simulation timestep"},hint:{gravity:"Try the Moon or Jupiter and compare the paths.",mass:"Does a heavier ball fly farther? Test it with and without air resistance.",airResistance:"Air pushes against the motion, so the path is no longer a perfect parabola.",timeStep:"A smaller step is more precise but needs more calculations."},measure:{x:"Horizontal position",y:"Height",vx:"Horizontal velocity",vy:"Vertical velocity",maxHeight:"Maximum height",range:"Distance",flightTime:"Flight time"},view:{maxHeight:"H =",range:"R ="},graph:{trajectory:"Trajectory (height vs distance)",position:"Position vs time",velocity:"Velocity vs time",energy:"Energy vs time"},challenge:{range50:{title:"Bullseye at 50 m",description:"Make the ball land exactly 50 m away (\xB10.5 m).",hint:"Start with 45\xB0 \u2014 it gives the longest distance on flat ground. Then adjust the speed."},height20:{title:"Reach 20 m high",description:"Make the ball reach a maximum height of 20 m (\xB10.3 m).",hint:"A steeper angle sends the ball higher. Only the vertical part of the speed matters for height."},lowAngle:{title:"The low road",description:"Land at 30 m (\xB10.5 m) using an angle smaller than 30\xB0.",hint:"Angles that add up to 90\xB0 (like 20\xB0 and 70\xB0) give the same distance at the same speed."},flight4:{title:"Four seconds in the air",description:"Keep the ball flying for exactly 4 s (\xB10.05 s).",hint:"Flight time depends on the vertical speed: T = 2\xB7v\u2080\xB7sin \u03B8 / g."},moon:{title:"Golf on the Moon",description:"Set the Moon's gravity (1.62 m/s\xB2) and land the ball at 100 m (\xB11 m).",hint:"Weaker gravity means longer flights. You will need much less speed than on Earth."},drag:{title:"Fighting the air",description:"With air resistance ON, land the ball at exactly 40 m (\xB10.5 m).",hint:"With air, the best angle is a little below 45\xB0. A heavier ball loses less speed."}}}},i={sim:a};export{i as default,a as sim};
